<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cynric</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cynric of Wessex</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cynric is named by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> as the son, or in some sources the grandson, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a> and his successor as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, ruling from 534 to 560. The Chronicle credits him with victories over the Britons at Old Sarum and Barbury and with taking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> with Cerdic. Like Cerdic, he is known only from later and partly legendary tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cynric.jpg" alt="Cynric" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: myself · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.circa.540.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Everything known about Cynric comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, which names him as the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a>, regarded as the founder of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. The Anglian king-list and parts of the West Saxon Genealogical Regnal List, which may partly derive from it and was a source for the Chronicle, say instead that he was the son of Cerdic's son Creoda. The genealogy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a>'s Life of King Alfred names Creoda, while Asser's account of Alfred's maternal ancestry calls Cynric son of Cerdic.</p><p>The name looks like Old English for kin-ruler, although its normal Old English form is Cyneric. Some scholars have proposed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a> and his successor Ceawlin had Celtic names, and an alternative etymology derives Cynric from the Brittonic Cunorix, meaning hound-king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Chronicle describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a> and Cynric landing with five ships near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a> in 495. It calls them aldormen and says they assumed rule over the Gewissae, as the West Saxons were known before the late seventh century, only in 519. This implies that Cynric was not at first a royal leader, and that he and his father became kings only after conquering the heartlands of the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>.</p><p>According to the Chronicle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> expanded into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a> under Cynric against strong resistance and captured Searobyrig, or Old Sarum, near Salisbury, in 552. In 556 he and his son Ceawlin won a battle against the Britons at Beranburh, now identified as Barbury Castle. If these dates are accurate, the earlier entries, beginning with the landing of 495, are unlikely to be correct. Ceawlin's origin and his relationship with Cynric are obscure. Chroniclers merely suggested that they were relatives, or that he was Cynric's son, to legitimise the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> line.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cynric” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, compiled in Wessex in the late ninth century, the kingdom was founded by Cerdic and his son <b>Cynric</b>, who landed on the south coast with five ships in 495 and in 519 ‘took the kingdom’ after a battle at Cerdicesford, probably Charford on the Avon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle claims that Cerdic and <b>Cynric</b> took the island in 530 and gave it to their kinsmen Stuf and Wihtgar, but the story is largely legendary.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-isle-of-wight">Kingdom of the Isle of Wight</a></li></ul></section>
